course: books
lesson: 24
title: Alice in Wonderland, chapter XII, part 2: Alice's Evidence (sentence first, a pack of cards, and waking up)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: The White Rabbit reads the verses: six muddled verses full of «he», «she», «they» and «you», where you can never tell who is who. The King calls them the most important evidence yet. Alice, who has grown so large she isn't a bit afraid, offers sixpence to anyone who can explain them: «I don't believe there's an atom of meaning in it.»
explain: The King finds «meaning» anyway: «said I could not swim» fits the Knave, who is made of cardboard; «they all returned from him to you» — why, there are the tarts on the table! When the Queen says she never had fits, he jokes «Then the words don't fit you», and nobody laughs until he explains it was a pun.
explain: «Let the jury consider their verdict,» says the King, for about the twentieth time. «No, no!» says the Queen. «Sentence first—verdict afterwards.» «Stuff and nonsense!» says Alice. «Off with her head!» screams the Queen — and nobody moves. Alice, now her full size, says: «Who cares for you? You're nothing but a pack of cards!»
explain: The whole pack flies up and comes down on her, and she finds herself lying on the river bank with her head in her sister's lap, while her sister brushes falling leaves off her face. It was all a dream. Alice tells her sister all her adventures, then runs in to tea. Her sister sits on, half-dreaming Wonderland too, and hears each strange noise turn into an ordinary farm sound; she pictures Alice grown up, telling these tales to children of her own.
explain: Looking back over the whole book: a bored girl followed a White Rabbit down a hole, grew and shrank, cried a pool of tears, met creatures who argued, gave orders and talked nonsense, and slowly learned to keep her temper, to hold her tongue when it would hurt, and in the end to stand up to unfairness and say «Nonsense!» — before waking to tea-time on a summer afternoon.
fact: The verses begin «They told me you had been to her, And mentioned me to him: She gave me a good character, But said I could not swim.» (a good character = a good report about someone).
fact: Another verse says «I gave her one, they gave him two, You gave us three or more».
fact: The six verses are full of «he», «she», «they», «you» and «it», so nobody can tell who anyone is; that is Carroll's joke about evidence that sounds important and means nothing.
fact: Alice, grown very large, says: «If any one of them can explain it, I'll give him sixpence. I don't believe there's an atom of meaning in it.» (sixpence = an old coin; an atom = the tiniest bit). The jury all write down «She doesn't believe there's an atom of meaning in it», but none of them tries to explain the paper.
fact: The King: «If there's no meaning in it, that saves a world of trouble, you know, as we needn't try to find any.»
fact: «You can't swim, can you?» the King asks the Knave; «Do I look like it?» — he is made entirely of cardboard.
fact: The King says «they all returned from him to you» means the tarts on the table, and «Nothing can be clearer than that».
fact: «You never had fits, my dear?» «Never!» says the Queen, throwing an inkstand at the Lizard; Bill uses the ink trickling down his face to write again.
fact: «Then the words don't fit you» is a pun on «fits» (sudden attacks of illness or temper) and «fit» (suit); there is dead silence until the King says «It's a pun!»
fact: The Queen: «Sentence first—verdict afterwards.» A verdict decides if someone is guilty; a sentence is the punishment. Alice: «Stuff and nonsense! The idea of having the sentence first!»
fact: «Hold your tongue!» — «I won't!» — «Off with her head!» — «Nobody moved.»
fact: «Who cares for you?» said Alice, (she had grown to her full size by this time.) «You're nothing but a pack of cards!»
fact: She wakes on the bank, her head in her sister's lap; the «cards» were dead leaves fluttering down from the trees onto her face.
fact: «Wake up, Alice dear! Why, what a long sleep you've had!» — «Oh, I've had such a curious dream!» Her sister sends her in to tea, «it's getting late».
fact: The sister sits on «with closed eyes», thinking of Alice's story, and «half believed herself in Wonderland».
fact: In the sister's half-dream, Wonderland's sounds turn back into real ones: the rattling teacups into tinkling sheep-bells, the Queen's shrill cries into the voice of the shepherd boy, the Mock Turtle's sobs into the lowing (mooing) of cattle.
fact: The sister pictures Alice as a grown woman who keeps «the simple and loving heart of her childhood» and tells other children strange tales, «perhaps even with the dream of Wonderland of long ago».
fact: Talk in this lesson: «cwtch» (Wales) = a cuddle; «our kid» (Manchester) = my brother or sister; «grand» (Ireland) = fine; «lekker» (South Africa) = great; «no cap» = honestly; «bussin'» = really good.
exercise: Who reads the verses aloud? | The White Rabbit | The King of Hearts himself | Alice, standing up | The Knave in chains
exercise: How many verses are there in the set the White Rabbit reads? | Six | Two, both short | Twelve, one for each juror | One
exercise: «She gave me a good character, But said I could not swim.» What does «a good character» mean here? | A good report about someone | A part in a play at the Queen's concert, with a costume | A nice handwriting | A good joke
exercise: What does the King call the verses? | The most important piece of evidence yet | Stuff and nonsense, to be thrown out of court | A pun | A letter to the Queen
exercise: What does Alice offer to anyone who can explain the verses? | Sixpence | A jam tart | Her thimble | A flamingo
exercise: Why is Alice not afraid to interrupt the King now? | She has grown so large in the last few minutes | The Gryphon is holding her hand tightly and whispering to her | The Queen likes her | She is about to leave
exercise: «I don't believe there's an atom of meaning in it.» What does «an atom» mean here? | The tiniest bit | A big, long scientific word that nobody understands | A deep, dark secret | A kind of jam tart
exercise: What do the jurors write down after Alice speaks? | «She doesn't believe there's an atom of meaning in it» | A clear explanation of all the verses, line by line | Her name | Nothing
exercise: «If there's no meaning in it,» says the King, what does that save? | A world of trouble, as they needn't try to find any | A great deal of money, a dish of tarts and the Knave's head | The Knave's head | Time for tea
exercise: «You can't swim, can you?» What does the Knave answer? | «Do I look like it?» | «Yes, very well» | «Only in the sea, with the Mock Turtle and the lobsters» | «I never learnt»
exercise: Why can't the Knave swim? | He is made entirely of cardboard | He was never taught swimming at school, as it was an extra | He is in chains | He is afraid of water
exercise: «They all returned from him to you.» What does the King point to? | The tarts on the table | The Queen | The jury-box | The Knave's crown on its crimson velvet cushion
exercise: «You never had fits, my dear?» What does the Queen do as she says «Never!»? | Throws an inkstand at the Lizard | Throws the whole dish of tarts at the King's head | Faints | Laughs
exercise: How does Bill the Lizard manage to write again? | He uses the ink trickling down his face | He finds his squeaky pencil | He borrows Alice's pencil and says thank you politely | He uses his tail
exercise: What happens after the King says «Then the words don't fit you»? | A dead silence, until he says «It's a pun!» and everybody laughs | Everyone laughs at once, before he explains it | The Queen bursts into tears and leaves the court in a fury | Alice leaves
exercise: «No, no! Sentence first—verdict afterwards.» Who says this? | The Queen | The King of Hearts | The White Rabbit | The Knave of Hearts
exercise: What does Alice say to «Sentence first»? | «Stuff and nonsense!» | «Yes, your Majesty, at once» | «Off with her own head» | «Consider your verdict»
exercise: «Hold your tongue!» What does Alice answer? | «I won't!» | «I'm sorry» | «Nonsense!» | «Who are you?»
exercise: What happens when the Queen shouts «Off with her head!» this time? | Nobody moves | The soldiers seize Alice at once and take her away | The King agrees | Alice runs away
exercise: «Who cares for you? You're nothing but a pack of cards!» What size is Alice now? | Her full size | More than a mile high, as Rule Forty-two says | Only three inches | About nine feet
exercise: What happens to the pack of cards after Alice says this? | It rises into the air and comes flying down upon her | It falls flat on the floor, face down | It vanishes slowly, like the Cheshire Cat, starting with the tail | It bows
exercise: Where does Alice find herself? | On the bank, her head in her sister's lap | In the long hall, holding the golden key, by the little door | At the March Hare's tea-table | At home in bed
exercise: What was really falling on Alice's face? | Dead leaves from the trees | Playing cards | Rain | Rose petals painted red by the gardeners
exercise: «Oh, I've had such a curious dream!» What does Alice's sister tell her to do? | Run in to her tea, as it is getting late | Go back to sleep on the bank and dream some more about Wonderland | Write it all down | Read her book
exercise: In her sister's half-dream, what do the rattling teacups change into? | Tinkling sheep-bells | Rustling grass in the wind by the pool | The voice of the shepherd boy | Cattle lowing in the farmyard
exercise: What does the Mock Turtle's sobbing turn into? | The lowing of the cattle in the distance | The rippling of the pool to the waving of the reeds | Sheep-bells | Wind in the grass
exercise: What does Alice's sister picture at the very end? | Alice grown up, keeping a loving child's heart and telling children strange tales | Alice as the new Queen of Hearts, with a crown on a velvet cushion | Alice going back down the rabbit-hole | Alice forgetting the dream for ever
test: Leo: «the verses were dead clear, proved the Knave did it, no cap.» (no cap = honestly) Put it right. | They were full of «he», «she» and «they» with no clear meaning; Alice said there wasn't an atom | Right: the verses named the Knave and the tarts in every line, and the King read them out proudly | They were signed by the Knave at the bottom in big letters | They were in French
test: «If there's no meaning in it, that saves a world of trouble.» What is wrong with the King's thinking when he then finds meaning anyway? | He decides what the verses mean to fit what he already wants | He reads them very carefully, line by line | He gives up | He asks Alice to explain them, and then listens carefully to her answer
test: «Do I look like it?» Why is the Knave's answer funny? | A cardboard card could never swim — but that doesn't prove he wrote the verses | The Knave is an excellent swimmer and is only joking with the King | He is a fish | He is very wet
test: Mia, our kid (my sister) says: «the King's pun got bare laughs straight off.» (bare = lots of) Put it right. | There was a dead silence, until he said «It's a pun!» | Right: everyone laughed at once | Nobody laughed ever | The Queen laughed first, loudly, and then everyone joined in
test: «Sentence first—verdict afterwards.» Why is this nonsense in a real trial? | You must decide if someone is guilty before you decide a punishment | The verdict and sentence are the same thing | A sentence is only a line of words | It's perfectly fair, and real courts always do it that way
test: What would have happened if the soldiers had obeyed «Off with her head!»? | The dream would have turned into a nightmare — but nobody moved | Alice would have turned into a playing card and joined the pack | The Knave would be freed | The King would be the judge
test: Why does nobody move when the Queen orders Alice's head off? | Alice is full-sized now and has seen they are «nothing but a pack of cards» | They are all asleep | They are deaf | They are waiting for the King to say it too, as the law says they must
test: Is this TRUE or FALSE? «Alice was really in Wonderland and came back up the rabbit-hole.» | False: she wakes on the bank; it was all a dream | True: she climbed all the way up the rabbit-hole and ran home to tea | True: the Gryphon flew her back | False: she never fell asleep
test: Ruby, grand (fine) about it: «the cards were really leaves blowing on her face, yeah?» Is she right? | Yes: dead leaves were fluttering down from the trees | No: they were real playing cards from her sister's pocket | No: it was rain | No: it was the cat
test: Why does the sister «half believe herself in Wonderland»? | She closes her eyes, thinking of Alice's story, and dreams it too | She falls down a rabbit-hole herself, after the White Rabbit | Alice tells her it was all true | She sees the White Rabbit
test: In the sister's dream, what real sound does the Queen's shrill voice turn into? | The voice of the shepherd boy | The tinkling of sheep-bells | The wind blowing through the long grass by the pool | The cattle
test: What does the ending say about growing up? | You can grow up and still keep a «simple and loving heart» and share stories | Growing up means forgetting all your dreams and never telling stories again | Grown-ups never dream | Only queens grow up
test: Looking back: in chapter I, what did Alice think «the use of a book» was without? | «Pictures or conversations» — and her own adventures are full of conversations | Long, grand words like «Latitude» and «Longitude» | Morals | Rules
test: Looking back: in chapter IV, stuck in the Rabbit's house, what did Alice say about a book? | «There ought to be a book written about me» | «I'll never read again» | «Books are useless» | «I'll write a book about Dinah when I get home»
test: Looking back: when did Alice first think «they're only a pack of cards, after all»? | When the procession reached her in the Queen's garden | At the tea-party | In the Duchess's kitchen, full of pepper | On the mushroom
test: Looking back: in chapter III, Fury the dog said «I'll be judge, I'll be jury... and condemn you to death». How is the Queen like Fury? | Both want the punishment decided before any fair trial | Both are dogs | Both love tarts and steal them on summer days | Both are kind
test: Looking back: how has Alice changed from the girl who cried a pool of tears in chapter II? | She now stands up for herself and says «I won't!» to the Queen | She now cries even more than before, and hides from everyone | She is smaller | She has forgotten Dinah
test: ★★★ The book begins and ends in the same place. Where, and doing what? | On the bank by her sister — bored at first, then waking from the dream | In the long hall, with the golden key in her hand, by the little door | At the tea-table | In the court
test: ★★★ «I gave her one, they gave him two, You gave us three or more.» If «three or more» is exactly three, how many things were given in all? | 6 | 3 | 5 | 1
test: ★★★ The sister's dream turns Wonderland noises back into real ones. Which pair is NOT one of them? | The Hatter's riddle into a bird singing | The teacups into sheep-bells | The Queen's cries into the shepherd boy | The Mock Turtle's sobs into the cattle lowing
exam: Identify the inference drawn by Alice concerning the verses produced in evidence. | That there was not an atom of meaning in them | That they demonstrated the Knave's guilt beyond doubt | That they were written by the Queen | That they concerned the art of swimming
exam: It is given that the Queen proposed «Sentence first—verdict afterwards». Select the response which most accurately characterises Alice's reply. | «Stuff and nonsense!» | «As your Majesty pleases» | «Consider your verdict» | Silence
exam: Ascertain the true nature of the objects which, in the dream, descended upon Alice as a pack of cards. | Dead leaves fluttering down from the trees | Playing cards from her sister's pocket | Rose petals painted red | Pages torn from her sister's book without pictures or conversations
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the conclusion of the narrative? | Alice awoke, related her dream, and was sent in to tea, whereupon her sister reflected on her future | Alice remained in Wonderland as its Queen | The Knave was beheaded | Alice's sister forbade her to dream again and sent her to bed without tea
